P.append(passage("ocs-02", "cp", "oc", 1, "Naming the Inhaled Anesthetics", [
    "Modern inhaled anesthetics are small halogenated ethers. Their brand names are short, but each has a systematic IUPAC name. In substitutive naming, an ether has no suffix of its own: the larger carbon chain is the parent, and the smaller side is named as an alkoxy prefix (for example, –OCHF<sub>2</sub> is difluoromethoxy). The chain is numbered to give the lowest set of locants to all the substituents.",
    structures("Figure 1 Three inhaled anesthetics", [
        ("FC(F)OC(Cl)C(F)(F)F", "Isoflurane"),
        ("FCOC(C(F)(F)F)C(F)(F)F", "Sevoflurane"),
        ("FC(F)OC(F)C(F)(F)F", "Desflurane")]),
    "Two properties guide their use. The blood/gas partition coefficient tells how much of the gas dissolves in blood compared with air at equilibrium. A gas that dissolves poorly in blood builds up its partial pressure in the brain quickly, so the patient falls asleep and wakes up fast. Potency is described by the minimum alveolar concentration (MAC), the inhaled percentage that keeps 50% of patients from moving in response to a surgical incision. A lower MAC means a more potent drug.",
    "More than a century ago, Meyer and Overton noticed that potency rises with solubility in olive oil, a stand-in for the lipid of nerve membranes. If their rule holds, the product MAC × (oil/gas coefficient) should be about the same for every agent.",
    "An anesthetist can switch quickly between agents, so the choice depends on the patient. A short outpatient procedure favors a drug that wears off fast; a long operation may favor a cheaper, more potent drug. Because the three molecules differ only in which halogens they carry and where, their names also show at a glance how similar they are.",
    table("Table 1 Properties at 37 °C (published average values)", ["Agent", "Blood/gas", "Oil/gas", "MAC (%)"],
          [["Isoflurane", "1.4", "98", "1.15"], ["Sevoflurane", "0.65", "47", "2.0"], ["Desflurane", "0.42", "19", "6.0"]]),
], [
    dict(q="What is the IUPAC name of desflurane?", right="2-(difluoromethoxy)-1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane",
         wrong=[("1-(difluoromethoxy)-1,2,2,2-tetrafluoroethane", "This numbers from the wrong end. Compare locant sets {1,1,1,2,2} and {1,1,2,2,2}: the first difference (1 vs 2) picks the CF<sub>3</sub> end as C1."),
                ("difluoromethyl tetrafluoroethyl ether", "This is a functional-class (common-style) name. The question asks for the substitutive IUPAC name."),
                ("1-(difluoromethoxy)-1,1,2,2-tetrafluoroethane", "This puts two F atoms on each carbon. Desflurane's ethane has three F on one carbon and one F on the other.")],
         e=["Parent: the two-carbon chain, ethane. The –OCHF<sub>2</sub> group is a difluoromethoxy prefix.",
            "Numbering from the CF<sub>3</sub> end gives F at 1,1,1,2 and the alkoxy group at 2, a locant set of {1,1,1,2,2}.",
            "The other direction gives {1,1,2,2,2}, which is higher at the first point of difference.",
            "Prefixes go in alphabetical order (d before t): 2-(difluoromethoxy)-1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane."], k=1,
         t="When locant sets tie on their first numbers, compare term by term. The first lower number wins."),
    dict(q="Which of the three anesthetics has NO chiral carbon?", right="Sevoflurane",
         wrong=[("Isoflurane", "Isoflurane's C2 holds four different groups: H, Cl, CF<sub>3</sub> and OCHF<sub>2</sub>. It is sold as a racemic mixture."),
                ("Desflurane", "Desflurane's C2 holds H, F, CF<sub>3</sub> and OCHF<sub>2</sub>, four different groups."),
                ("All three are chiral", "Sevoflurane's central carbon carries two identical CF<sub>3</sub> groups, so it is not a stereocenter.")],
         e=["A chiral carbon needs four different groups.",
            "In sevoflurane the CH carbon holds H, OCH<sub>2</sub>F and two CF<sub>3</sub> groups. Two groups match, so it is achiral."], k=1),
    dict(q="Based on Table 1, which agent would let a patient wake up fastest after surgery?", right="Desflurane",
         wrong=[("Isoflurane", "Isoflurane has the highest blood/gas coefficient (1.4), so the most drug stays dissolved in blood and it clears slowest."),
                ("Sevoflurane", "Sevoflurane (0.65) is faster than isoflurane, but desflurane (0.42) is lower still."),
                ("The agent with the lowest MAC", "MAC measures potency, not speed. The lowest MAC belongs to isoflurane, the slowest agent.")],
         e=["Speed of onset and recovery depends on the blood/gas coefficient: lower means faster.",
            "Desflurane has the lowest value, 0.42."], k=4),
    dict(q="A new ether has an oil/gas partition coefficient of 60. If it follows the Meyer–Overton rule like the agents in Table 1, its MAC should be closest to",
         right="1.8%", wrong=[("0.6%", "This uses 60/100. MAC goes down as oil solubility goes up, so MAC ≈ constant ÷ (oil/gas)."),
                               ("6.0%", "That is desflurane's MAC, which has an oil/gas value of only 19. A more oil-soluble agent is more potent."),
                               ("60%", "MAC × oil/gas is about 100 (not 1) for the agents in Table 1.")],
         e=["MAC × oil/gas: isoflurane 1.15 × 98 ≈ 113; sevoflurane 2.0 × 47 = 94; desflurane 6.0 × 19 = 114. The average is about 107.",
            "New agent: MAC ≈ 107 ÷ 60 ≈ 1.8%."], k=4,
         t="When a passage says a product should be constant, calculate it for each row first, then use the average."),
    dict(q="A researcher wants to test whether anesthetic potency depends on lipid solubility rather than on molecular size. Which pair of test compounds would be most useful?",
         right="Two compounds with similar molar mass but very different oil/gas coefficients",
         wrong=[("Two compounds with similar oil/gas coefficients but different blood/gas coefficients", "This varies blood solubility, which affects speed, not potency. Lipid solubility is held constant, so it can't be tested."),
                ("Two compounds that differ in both molar mass and oil/gas coefficient", "If both change, any difference in MAC can't be traced to one of them. The variables are confounded."),
                ("The two enantiomers of isoflurane", "Enantiomers have the same molar mass and the same oil/gas coefficient, so neither variable changes.")],
         e=["To test one variable, change only that variable and hold the other constant.",
            "Matching molar mass removes size as an explanation, so a difference in MAC can be linked to oil solubility."], k=3),
]))

